TITLE:: RLRegions
summary:: The keys round one tonic: Schoenberg's chart of the regions
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLScale, Classes/RLFit, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
One tonality, and every other key a region of it, nearer or farther (Schoenberg, emphasis::Structural Functions of Harmony::, chapters III, IV and IX). A region is code::[fifths, mode]::: where its tonic stands on the line of fifths from the central tonic, and code::\major:: or code::\minor::. On the line of fifths G sharp (8) and A flat (-4) are two regions, as on the chart, whatever the tuning makes of them.

The chart names the regions and puts them in five classes of relationship.

table::
## round a major tonic || 1 direct and close || D, SD, sm, m
## || 2 indirect but close || t, sd, v, SM, M, bM, bSM
## || 3 indirect || bm, bsm, MM, Mm, bsmSM, bsmsm
## || 4 indirect and remote || Np, dor, S/T, bMD, bmv
## || 5 distant || every other region
## round a minor tonic || 1 close || M, T, v, sd
## || 2 indirect but close || D, SM
## || 3 indirect || m, sm, SD
## || 4 indirect and remote || #sm, #SM, #m, #M, subT, subt, Np
## || 5 distant || every other region
::

The classes are Schoenberg's judgement of the practice up to the end of the 19th century, not a measure. Counted on Beethoven's piano sonatas (the key left as the tonic), classes 1 and 2 hold nearly nine changes of key in ten from a major key, three in four from a minor one.

code::
~r = RLRegions(RLEDO(12), 0, \major);
~r.region(\sm);                          // [3, \minor]: A minor
~r.region(\SMsm);                        // [6, \minor]: the submediant major's submediant minor
~r.classOf([-4, \major]);                // 2: A flat major, bSM
~r.classOf([8, \major]);                 // 3: G sharp major, MM
~r.relation(\D, \sm);                    // 4: A minor seen from G major is its dor
~r.scale(\sm);                           // RLScale: A minor
::

subsection:: Regions of any mode: the context of modes

A region may be of any diatonic mode (link::Classes/RLScale#*modes::): code::[1, \mixolydian]:: is the Mixolydian on G. Major and minor regions are two of the seven; the chart knows only them. The modes make one context. A region there is a centre and a signature (which seven fifths its tones are), and two regions stand apart by an interval, code::[how many fifths the centre moves, how many the signature does]::. Three relations generate it:

table::
## R || the same tones about another centre || C major, D Dorian, G Mixolydian, A minor
## S || the same centre, one tone changed || C major, C Mixolydian
## T || the same mode a fifth away || G Mixolydian, D Mixolydian
::

How many of these steps part two regions is their distance, and the class of a region where the chart has no word. It measures tones in common, not Schoenberg's relationships: the parallel minor is three steps from a major key, and on the chart in class 2.

code::
RLRegions.interval(\major, 1, \mixolydian);     // [1, 0]: G Mixolydian has C major's tones, a fifth up
RLRegions.interval(\major, 0, \mixolydian);     // [0, -1]: C Mixolydian is C major with one flat more
RLRegions.steps(\major, 2, \dorian);            // 1: D Dorian from C major
RLRegions.classBetween(\major, 0, \dorian);     // 2: C Dorian, two tones changed
~r.scale([2, \dorian]).degrees;                 // [2, 4, 5, 7, 9, 11, 0]
~ctx = ~r.context(2);                           // a relation context: 35 regions, R, S, T
~ctx.graph.distance([0, \major], [0, \dorian]); // 2
~ctx.act(\R, [0, \major]);                      // the six other modes with C major's tones
::

A reading uses them through its style prior (link::Classes/RLStylePrior#-modes::): link::Classes/RLFit:: offers the modes the prior names.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: system
argument:: tonic
A pitch class of the system (its zero when nil).
argument:: mode

METHOD:: majorChart
METHOD:: minorChart
The charts: code::[name, fifths, mode, class]:: for each named region.
METHOD:: chart

METHOD:: classBetween
The class (0 the same key, 1 to 5) of a key of code::toMode:: whose tonic is code::fifths:: from the tonic of a key of code::fromMode::. Between major and minor keys: the chart. For a region of another mode, or for every region when code::byModes:: is true: its link::#*steps:: in the context of modes (5 for more).
METHOD:: nameBetween
Its name on the chart, or nil.

METHOD:: below
A mode's place among the seven (link::Classes/RLScale#*below::).
METHOD:: third
code::\major:: or code::\minor::: the mode of a mode's tonic triad. A Mixolydian region is a major one (the written key G major is met by the Mixolydian on G), a Dorian a minor.
METHOD:: charted
Whether the chart knows the mode (major and minor).
METHOD:: interval
The interval between two regions in the context of modes: code::[centre, signature]::, in fifths.
METHOD:: steps
How many steps of R, S and T part them.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: region
A region from its name: one symbol of the chart, or several in a row, each read from the region before. A region is returned as it is.

METHOD:: relation
The class of a region seen from another.
METHOD:: classOf
Seen from the central tonic.
METHOD:: nameOf

METHOD:: tonicOf
A region's tonic as a pitch class of the system.
METHOD:: scale
Its link::Classes/RLScale::.

METHOD:: regions
Every region within code::span:: fifths of the tonic: major and minor, or the code::modes:: named.

METHOD:: context
The context of modes as an link::Classes/RLRelationContext::: the regions within code::span:: fifths (every mode when none is named) under R, S and T. Its graph gives the distances (link::#*steps::, away from the span's edge); a piece's regions are a walk on it.
METHOD:: names
METHOD:: central
